Round d1_r7 complete. Summary of what was done:

**The change**: My r6 first-call placement probe picked candidates by *min over 3 short bursts* (13–60 µs), but the driver scores the *median of ≥20 ms samples* — d1_race's r6 record measured exactly this mismatch ("burst-fast/steady-slow" draws), and my 31 B1 chain board regression (0.0511 → 0.0580, all 3 scoring processes steadily slow) was the confirming symptom. Both probes now score each candidate by the median of 5 interleaved samples, each calibrated to ~275 µs of work, and the candidate count went from 4 to 6 per path (extra nop-pad code copies and stack-shift variants). Arithmetic untouched — verified two-process bitwise-identical output on all 8 graded cells with the probes picking different candidates per process. I also established by code comparison that batchlane's 31 B1 chain lead is not structural (their step carries more adds than mine), which is what justified fixing the statistic instead of rewriting the kernel.

**Verification on the scoring node** (a80n0, leased core, interleaved r6-vs-r7 process pairs): 13 B1 exec won 5 of 6 pairs (per-process medians ~0.015 vs 0.018 µs — the worst r6 cell); every other cell at parity with no probe tax; all correctness gates pass (single-call 1.4–4.2e-16 at L=7/11/13/17/31 across batches, all chain gates with 3+ decades of margin, strict m=2, odd batches). Strategy record appended; only `impl/d1_prime.c` and `strategies/d1_prime.md` were written; slot lease released.

**One line as requested**: Technique — driver-statistic placement probe (median-of-5 ~275 µs samples over 6 byte-identical candidates, adopted from d1_race r6); best scoring-node numbers (a80n0; wallaby not used for timing this round): B=1 0.014 µs at L=13 / 0.046 µs at L=31, batched B=512 0.009 µs at L=13 / 0.042 µs at L=31 (chains 0.034/0.014/0.051/0.044); rel L2 ≤ 4.2e-16.
